      "id": "01",
      "title": "The lowbit trick",
      "question": "In a Fenwick tree, which elements does t[i] cover?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Fenwick tree, question one. A Fenwick tree is a single array, t. Which elements does t of i cover? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Which elements does <em>t[i]</em> cover?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Here are eight values. Each slot, t of i, holds the sum of a block that ends at i, and the block's length is the lowest set bit of i. Odd indices end in a one bit, so they cover just one element. Two and six end in one zero: their lowest bit is two, so they cover two elements each. Four is one zero zero: it covers four elements, one through four. And eight covers all eight. Every prefix splits into at most log n of these blocks.",
