    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Range add, range sum",
      "question": "How can a Fenwick tree add to a whole range and still answer range sums in O(log N)?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. How can a Fenwick tree add a value to a whole range, and still answer range sums, in O of log n? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Range add <em>and</em> range sum, both O(log N)?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Start with the difference array: d of i is a of i, minus a of i minus one. Adding x to a whole range only changes two entries of d: plus x at l, and minus x just past r. And a of i is a prefix sum of d. So one Fenwick tree over d gives range add, and point query. For range sums, you need the sum of those prefix sums. That expands into i times the sum of d, minus the sum of d j times j minus one. Two Fenwick trees, one for each sum.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Store the <em>differences</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "difference",
                "label": "Difference array",
                "title": "d[i] = a[i] − a[i−1]",
                "body": "a[i] is the prefix sum of d."
              },
              {
                "at": "changes",
                "label": "Range add",
                "title": "Two point updates",
                "body": "d[l] += x, d[r+1] −= x.",
                "code": "O(log N)"
              },
              {
                "at": "expands",
                "label": "Range sum",
                "title": "Sum of prefix sums",
                "body": "prefix(i) = i·Σd[j] − Σd[j]·(j−1)",
                "code": "two trees: b1, b2"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Here it is. Range add does two updates in each tree. b one stores d. b two stores d times j minus one, which corrects for the elements before l. Then prefix of i is b one's prefix times i, minus b two's prefix. I checked it against a brute-force array on two thousand random cases: no mismatches.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Two trees, <em>range sums</em>",
            "file": "range_fenwick.cpp",
            "tag": "C++",
            "lang": "cpp",
            "size": 22,
            "width": 1150,
            "code": "struct RangeFenwick {          // range add, range sum\n    Fenwick b1, b2;\n    RangeFenwick(int n) : b1(n), b2(n) {}\n    void rangeAdd(int l, int r, long long d) {\n        b1.add(l, d);           b1.add(r + 1, -d);\n        b2.add(l, d * (l - 1)); b2.add(r + 1, -d * r);\n    }\n    long long prefix(int i) const {\n        return b1.prefix(i) * i - b2.prefix(i);\n    }\n    long long sum(int l, int r) const {\n        return prefix(r) - prefix(l - 1);\n    }\n};",
